# Phase 18 queue Default-adapter reverse audit — 2026-10-23T02:11:53Z

Branch: `phase/18-phase-18-compatibility-closure`
Commit: `6371afa5` (`test(consumer): audit queue Default adapters`)
Pinned Java source: `efa1be59d6fc747ad220f036c86030f05f0419f9`.
Base environment: macOS AArch64, Apple M5 Max, Rust 1.85.0.

## Decision and change

The reverse API audit already mapped `ProcessQueue::new()` and
`PopProcessQueue::new()` to Java no-argument constructors, but omitted each
type's public Rust `Default` implementation. Source inspection confirmed both
`Default::default()` implementations call `Self::new()`. The crosswalk now
records those aliases as idiomatic Rust construction adapters with no extra
queue capability. The facade regression compares their stable initial flags,
ready-message state, and counters to `new()`.

## Checks

- `cargo test --offline -p rocketmq-classic-consumer --test facade_api_surface --locked` — 17 passed.
- `cargo test --offline -p rocketmq-classic-consumer --all-targets --all-features --locked` — 260 library tests and 74 integration tests passed.
- `cargo clippy --offline -p rocketmq-classic-consumer --all-targets --all-features --locked -- -D warnings` — passed.
- `cargo fmt --all -- --check` and `git diff --check` — passed.

The initial draft compared constructor timestamps and correctly exposed a
1-millisecond construction-time difference. Those transient fields were
removed from the comparison; the test now compares stable queue state only.
An initial typed tuple-list draft also failed to compile because the snapshot
mixes boolean and integer fields; it was changed to typed per-field
assertions. Final checks pass.

This closes only the two queue `Default` reverse-audit rows. Broad P18-V01
source/API/settings/input/error/control-flow and reverse-export reconciliation
remains PARTIAL. Remote Ubuntu CI, native Windows runtime execution, and Phase
19 Broker/Docker evidence remain open.
